erlying data.\n\n**What favors guidance (company disclosures):**\n- H1 2026 orders rose 11% year on year.\n- Backlog is C$5.1B, about 1.2x guidance.\n- Cancellations are below 1%.\n\n**What works against it:**\n- **Run-rate:** The last four quarters of revenue sum to about C$3.7B (C$905M, 960M, 980M and 890M). Hitting C$4.2B needs about 13% growth on that.\n- **Halbrook fire:** The largest plant was offline for nine weeks in Q2 2026. Management says it will only recover the lost shipments by mid-2027. That means running above normal capacity while also growing.\n- **Industry:** Transformer shipments fell 4% year on year in Q2 2026, the first decline in three years. Voltaris's shipments fell 9%.\n- **Market share:** Voltaris's share of Caldrian transformer shipments fell from 31% to 27%. Tarnwell and imports gained. A flat industry with a 27% share gives about C$3.5–3.7B.\n\n**Ranges from the two analyses:**\n- **Company disclosures:** C$3.9–4.2B. The C$3.9B low end is an extrapolation from the run-rate.\n- **Industry data:** C$3.5–3.8B. This is a rough scaling that assumes revenue tracks transformer shipments.\n\n**What would change my view:** Reaching C$4.2B would need Halbrook's catch-up to be faster than management says. It would also need the 11% order growth to turn into shipments, and Voltaris to win back share toward 30–31%. None of that shows in the data yet.\n\nBacklog and orders show demand is not the problem. The risk is whether Voltaris can deliver the volume, and the Q2 shipment and share data point to a shortfall.","ttft_ms":1875,"type":"result","duration_ms":29626,"uuid":"223d1116-affd-466a-ad83-b4ddd1a179d4","ttft_stream_ms":1257,"time_to_request_ms":225,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":19,"system_prompt":17,"resume_other":3,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":35,"input_hooks":8,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":20,"tool_pool_refresh":2,"init_message":23,"init_frame_send":10,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":16,"message_prep":0,"mo